c"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"1 v. (various pagings) ; 23 cm."},"tableOfContents":"Introduction to and commentary on John Macksoud's Other Illusions: Inquiries Toward a Rhetorical Theory / Craig R. Smith -- Other illusions : inquiries toward a rhetorical theory / S. John Macksoud -- Toward a rhetorical theory of language : parallels between the work of John Macksoud and Jacques Derrida / Gregory Desilet.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"by Craig R. Smith."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references."}],"subject":{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Rhetoric","Philosophy"]},"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"PN175 .M115 2009"},{"@edition":"22","@authority":"ddc","#text":"808/.001"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Philosophy/communication series"}},{"titleInfo":{"title":"Philosophy/communication"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Philosophy/communication"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990121630460203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781557535153"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"1557535159"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^2009007087"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"261177485"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"PN175 .M115 2009"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Widener"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990121630460203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"012163046"}},"processingDate":"2023-10-30T05:22Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"aacr","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"DLC"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"090225"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20100222"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990121630460203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.6 using MARC21slim2MODS3-6.xsl\n        (Revision 1.117 2017/02/14)"}},{"titleInfo":{"title":"Is faith in God reasonable?","subTitle":"debates in philosophy, science, and rhetoric"},"name":[{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Gould, Paul M.",{"@type":"date","#text":"1971-"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"editor."}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Miller, Corey",{"@type":"date","#text":"1972-"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"editor."}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Craig, William Lane","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"debate participant."}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Rosenberg, Alexander",{"@type":"date","#text":"1946-"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"debate participant."}}}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2014"},"issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York :"}},"publisher":"Routledge,","dateIssued":"2014."}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"viii, 177 pages ; 24 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"The question of whether faith in God is reasonable is of renewed interest in today's academy. In light of this interest, as well as the rise of militant religion and terrorism and the emergent reaction by neo-atheism, this volume considers this important question from the views of contemporary scientists, philosophers, and in a more novel fashion, of rhetoricians. It is comprised of a public debate (transcript in Part I) at Purdue University, West Lafayette, Indiana, on February 1, 2013 between William Lane Craig, supporting the position that faith in God is reasonable and Alex Rosenberg, arguing against that position.Scholars in the aforementioned fields then respond to the debate, representing both theistic and atheistic positions. The book concludes with rejoinders from Craig and Rosenberg. EDITORS: Corey Miller is Adjunct Professor of Philosophy and Comparative Religions at Indiana University-Kokomo. Paul Gould received his PhD in philosophy from Purdue University and is Adjunct Professor at Southeastern Baptist Theological Seminary. Publisher's note."},"tableOfContents":"Part I. Introduction and debate. 1. Introduction : faith, reason, and God / Corey Miller and Paul Gould -- 2. The debate : Is faith in God reasonable? / William Lane Craig vs. Alex Rosenberg -- -- Part II. Respondents. 3. God and the rational basis for science / Robert Kaita -- 4. Faith in anything is unreasonable / Victor J. Stenger -- 5. God without argument / Paul K. Moser -- 6. Some philosophical aspects of the Craig-Rosenberg debate / Theodore M. Drange -- 7. There are serious reasons for belief in the existence of God / Timothy McGrew -- 8. Not reasonable but not unreasonable / Michael Ruse -- 9. Rhetoric as hermeneutic key : the reasonableness of faith in God / Martin J. Medhurst -- 10. Faith, not reason, underwrites the belief in God / Clarke Rountree -- -- Part III. Rejoinders. 11. Reply to our respondents : an eminently reasonable faith / William Lane Craig -- 12. Replies to critics : very brief, very selective, rather snarky / Alex Rosenberg.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"edited by Corey Miller and Paul Gould."},"Includes index.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Faith"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Faith and reason"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Faith and reason","Christianity"]},{"@authority":"gnd","topic":"Glaube"},{"@authority":"gnd","topic":"Vernunft"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Faith"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Faith and reason"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":["Faith and reason","Christianity"]}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"BT771.3 .I8 2014"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"212"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Routledge studies in the philosophy of religion ; 9"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Routledge studies in the philosophy of religion ; 9"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990141611620203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780415709408"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0415709407"},{"@type":"isbn","@invalid":"yes","#text":"9781315885544 (ebk)"},{"@type":"isbn","@invalid":"yes","#text":"1315885549 (ebk)"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^2013049583"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"866614620"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000121705139","#text":"Harvard Divinity School Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BT771.3 .I8 2014"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BT771.3 .I8 2014"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":["Divinity Library","Widener"]},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990141611620203941","priorrecordids"